### How to cite

Use this, or the equivalent in whatever format your context calls for:

> *The AI Engineer Interview Register*. 4Geeks Academy, 2026. Licensed CC BY 4.0.
> https://github.com/breatheco-de/ai-engineer-interview-questions

A machine-readable citation is in [CITATION.cff](CITATION.cff). GitHub turns it into a
"Cite this repository" button in the sidebar.

Where the credit has to appear:

| If you | Then |
|---|---|
| Quote a number, a finding or a figure | Name 4Geeks Academy in the text or the caption, and link to this repository. |
| Republish questions from the dataset | Carry the credit **and** the source row — every question records the `source_url`, `source_author` and `evidence_tier` it came from. |
| Build a course, a bootcamp or a study guide on it | Credit 4Geeks Academy where students can see it, not only in a repository footer. |
| Fork or redistribute the dataset | Keep this LICENSE file intact and state what you changed. |
| Train on it or feed it to a model | The same attribution applies; a derived dataset carries this credit forward. |

### On the question texts themselves

The question texts were collected from public posts on X, two interviewer blogs, and three published
question banks. Every row in `data/questions.csv` carries the `source_url`, `source_author` and
`source_date` it came from, and the `evidence_tier` that says what kind of source that is.

The compilation, the unification, the labeling, the analysis and the writing are 4Geeks Academy's
contribution, and that is what is licensed here. The underlying posts belong to the people who wrote
them. Crediting 4Geeks Academy does not release you from crediting them: if you republish a
question, carry its source row with it.

